  "worked_solution": "We need to express the summation $S(k+1)$ in terms of $S(k).$\n\nLet ${a}_{i}$ for $i\\ge 1$ be a sequence, and let $S(k)$ denote the sum of the first $k$ terms.\n\n$$\nS(k)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{a}_{i}\n$$\n\nWe can always express $S(k+1)$ in terms of $S(k)$ by writing the last term on its own, as follows: \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}{a}_{i}=({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{a}_{i})+{a}_{k+1}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${a}_{i}=6i+1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k+1}=6(k+1)+1=6k+7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}(6i+1)=({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}(6i+1))+(6k+7),\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as \n\n$$\nS(k+1)=S(k)+6k+7.\n$$",
